WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.923
As a girl in Havana, Celia Cruz earned her

2
00:00:02.923 --> 00:00:05.733
first pair of shoes by singing for a tourist.

3
00:00:06.083 --> 00:00:10.217
Fifty years later she gave her stage shoes to the Smithsonian.

4
00:00:10.567 --> 00:00:16.464
She was born in Havana on 21 October 1925, into a poor family.

5
00:00:16.814 --> 00:00:19.324
According to the Smithsonian, she was one of

6
00:00:19.324 --> 00:00:22.120
fourteen children, and her singing began at home.

7
00:00:22.470 --> 00:00:24.815
That voice soon reached bigger audiences.

8
00:00:25.165 --> 00:00:28.648
She went on to sing with the Cuban band La Sonora Matancera.

9
00:00:28.998 --> 00:00:32.841
Soon after Fidel Castro took power in 1959, Cruz

10
00:00:32.841 --> 00:00:36.603
was touring in Mexico with La Sonora Matancera.

11
00:00:36.953 --> 00:00:37.975
She did not go home.

12
00:00:38.324 --> 00:00:41.824
Most accounts date her departure to 1960

13
00:00:41.824 --> 00:00:45.499
and her move to the United States to 1961.

14
00:00:45.849 --> 00:00:48.055
According to her biographers, Castro's

15
00:00:48.055 --> 00:00:50.668
government barred her from returning to Cuba.

16
00:00:51.018 --> 00:00:53.704
She made her life in New York and New Jersey,

17
00:00:53.704 --> 00:00:56.509
and sang about Cuba for the rest of her career.

18
00:00:56.859 --> 00:00:58.663
She recorded and performed with

19
00:00:58.663 --> 00:01:01.515
musicians such as Tito Puente and Johnny Pacheco.

20
00:01:01.865 --> 00:01:05.327
On 14 October 1994, on the South Lawn of the White

21
00:01:05.327 --> 00:01:09.620
House, President Clinton presented the National Medal of Arts.

22
00:01:09.970 --> 00:01:10.585
He said:

23
00:01:11.185 --> 00:01:14.297
“Celia Cruz has used her powerful voice and style to

24
00:01:14.297 --> 00:01:17.768
transplant Afro-Cuban music to every corner of the globe.”

25
00:01:18.368 --> 00:01:21.855
In 1997, at a ceremony marking fifty years of her

26
00:01:21.855 --> 00:01:25.485
musical career, she gave a stage costume, shoes and

27
00:01:25.485 --> 00:01:29.897
all, to the Smithsonian's National Museum of American History.

28
00:01:30.247 --> 00:01:33.023
In 2024 the United States Mint put her on a

29
00:01:33.023 --> 00:01:36.574
quarter as part of its American Women Quarters program.

30
00:01:36.924 --> 00:01:40.674
If you could never go home again, which song or sound would you carry with you?
